#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run a CPU-heavy command when it's its turn and its cores are quiet.

    heavy -- CMD [ARGS...]                a build, a test suite, anything heavy
    heavy --long -- CMD [ARGS...]         a long run, such as a local mutants shard
    heavy --share -- CMD [ARGS...]        a build with no time limits: it may share a
                                          lane
    heavy --alone -- CMD [ARGS...]        a timing: no job shares its lane
    heavy --few-seconds -- CMD [ARGS...]  seconds of work, such as a few tests: at once
                                          on quiet cores, else next
    heavy status                          each lane's jobs and how busy its cores are
    heavy speed                           the machine's usual speed, by vCPU and load
    heavy history [--since TIME] [--overlapping PID|TIME|START..END]
                                          the jobs it ran: when, for whom, how
    heavy waits [--since TIME] [--lane normal|long] [--min-jobs N] [--json]
                                          how long jobs waited for their turn, and
                                          how many wait now
    heavy waits --until-open [--p90 S] [--waiting N] [--max-wait S] [--lane L]
                                          wait for the queue to be quiet enough to
                                          start work
    heavy waits --until-quiet [--load N] [--max-wait S]
                                          wait for an empty queue on a quiet
                                          machine, as before a timing

Every session on this VM shares two lanes, each running one job at a time at full
priority, first come first served. A long job runs on the last quarter of the cores;
while one runs or waits, a normal job gets the rest, and otherwise all of them, so a
commit's checks never wait behind a long run. (With fewer than 4 cores, a long job takes
the normal lane.) The head of a lane starts once its cores have stayed under half busy
at every one-second sample for 10 seconds, however long that takes: work outside the
queue holds it up, and `heavy status` shows how busy the cores are. A job that arrives
to a free lane, nobody waiting, starts at the first quiet sample. CMD then runs in the
foreground, pinned to those cores (CARGO_BUILD_JOBS set to match), with this call's
environment, folder, terminal and output, and heavy exits with its status. Commit checks
join the queue on their own, through agent-commit-gate. wt-ship takes one job for a
landing's check when it needs the queue, and while it holds a main tree's claim, the job
joins at the queue's front, ahead of every waiter that didn't (first come first served
among those that did): it doesn't stop the running job, but goes next.

A --few-seconds job is seconds of work, such as a few tests after an edit, marked so on
trust. heavy first watches the normal lane's cores for HEAVY_QUICK_SECONDS. When they
average under HEAVY_QUICK_BUSY busy and 4 GiB of memory are free, CMD runs at once,
outside the queue, whether or not a job holds the lane, unless that job runs --alone: it
takes no slot and joins no queue, and runs on the normal lane's cores at nice 10, as a
sharer does, so a holder keeps most of any core both want, and first in line for the OOM
killer. It's niced even on a free lane, since a job may take the lane while it runs. A
--few-seconds job that takes the slot runs as any holder does, at full priority.
Otherwise it joins the queue's front, as wt-ship's job does, and keeps watching at each
poll: once the cores have averaged that quiet over the window, it leaves the queue and
runs outside it. Heading the lane with the slot free, it starts at the first sample
under half busy, not after HEAVY_QUIET_SECONDS of them, which would cost it more than
its run, and it never hears the PR hint. A --few-seconds job that ran past
HEAVY_QUICK_MAX seconds hears once, as it ends, to run ones like it, or the build before
them, without --few-seconds; nothing is stopped. A --few-seconds call may run in the
foreground, but the running job can take minutes to end, so give the Bash call the
tool's longest timeout (600000 ms); heavy-gate refuses the call without it.
--few-seconds goes with none of --long, --alone and --share: each pair is a usage error
that says why.

A lane holds one more job when its holder leaves cores idle. The first waiter in line
that asks to (--share) and fits starts beside the holder at nice 10, so the holder
keeps about 90% of any core both want, and the sharer can run several times slower
than alone. So share only work with no wall-clock limits, such as a build: many test
suites have them, from the runner (pytest-timeout, nextest's slow-timeout) or in the
tests themselves (a wait with a deadline), and fail on a busy core for no fault of the
code. Commit checks never share. A waiter fits when 4 GiB of memory are free and
either:
  - heavy has seen both commands exit 0 before, and the waiter's usual cores plus the
    holder's (its usual or its last 10 seconds, whichever is more) come to at most
    three quarters of the holder's cores; or
  - the holder's cores have averaged under 30% busy for 90 seconds.
The sharer runs on the holder's cores, first in line for the OOM killer, with
CARGO_BUILD_JOBS at the cores the holder leaves of that three quarters. It stays
niced to the end, since only root can undo a nice. A job run with --alone never
shares, and no job shares beside it: time things with it.

Work outside the queue can still share an --alone job's cores once it has started, so
when one ends, pass or fail, heavy says on stderr how many CPU-seconds other work used
on its cores while it ran, and how many cores that is on average: their busy time in
/proc/stat less the job's own (CMD and what it waited for; a process CMD left running
counts as other work) and less what sccache's server used meanwhile, which the line
names: the server runs a cargo job's compiles but isn't its child, and since every
session shares it, another session's compiles then count as the job's. Past
HEAVY_ALONE_OUTSIDE cores the line warns that the timing may be off. It names the
host's steal on those cores meanwhile, when there was any, and the machine's speed at
the job's start and end (below), once heavy knows its usual speed; at under 0.8x at
both, it says the timing isn't one to trust. A measure that can't be taken is a note,
and CMD's status stands. HEAVY_ALONE_REPORT names a file that gets the numbers as one
line, `seconds own_cpu_seconds other_cpu_seconds start_speed end_speed` (each speed
`-` when unknown), for a tool that times runs.

The host can throttle this VM's CPU, and the sandbox can't see it otherwise: cgroup
cpu.stat, /proc/cpuinfo and cpufreq showed nothing the afternoon it did (2026-11-26),
when the owner said of a timing "The CPU was throttled really low, so I wouldn't trust
that 262 s number right now". So after every job frees its place, heavy starts a probe
of the machine's speed in a child of its own session and exits without waiting for it:
the child measures the load over the next second (HEAVY_POLL), then times a fixed CPU
loop, 4 runs of about 8 ms each on a quiet machine, pinned to the least busy of the
job's cores, takes the load while the loop ran instead when that was busier, as when
the next job has ramped up meanwhile, writes the result, and exits, within 10 s at most
(its own alarm stops it then), so it needs no stopping. While one runs, a job that ends
starts none. The job itself is judged by the newest probe of the last 5 minutes whose
speed is known, any job's or cron's, and with none counts as at an unknown speed. In
the sandbox, `machine-speed probe` also probes every 5 minutes from cron (install.py),
so a job that runs past 5 minutes while no other job ends is judged too; where that
cron job isn't installed, such a job counts at its raw time. An
--alone job probes inline instead, as it starts, reusing the last second of its wait
for the load, and as it ends, before it frees its place. The machine's speed factor is
the loop's usual process time over its time now: 1.0 is the usual speed, 0.4 is 2.5
times slower. Load slows the loop too on this power-bound VM (16 ms at a load average
of 1.5), so usual is per vCPU and load bucket, the load being the machine's busy vCPUs,
steal left out: under 1, 1-3, 3-6 or 6+. It is the fastest tenth of the last 3 days'
probes on this machine, this vCPU and this bucket, from at least 20 of them; with
fewer, the speed is unknown. `heavy speed` prints them. The probes go on a file beside
the perf promises' logs (lib/machinespeed.py says where and how), and a probe or file
that fails is at most the job's one perf-promises note.

While machine-speed's cron probes hold a slow spell open (3 in a row under 0.25x),
`heavy status` and `heavy waits` lead with one line saying how many times slower than
usual the host CPU runs and to check the host for throttling (PROCHOT); `waits`
prints it on stderr, so its stdout stays one line, or JSON.

A command's usual cores come from its last 5 runs that exited 0: the most CPU time
any of them used per second of its run, counting CMD and the processes it waited for.
Every worktree of a repo shares one history, in HEAVY_DIR/history.json.

While it waits, heavy prints a line on stderr when what it waits on changes. When it
joins a lane that already holds two jobs, or has headed its lane for
HEAVY_STALL_SECONDS without quiet cores, it suggests getting build and test results
from GitHub instead: push a topic branch and open a PR (never main).

Every job heavy runs, in a slot or outside the queue, goes on a record,
HEAVY_DIR/jobs.jsonl, as a JSON line when it starts and another when it ends
(lib/heavyjobs.py lists the fields): when it joined the queue, started and ended; its
exit status, or the signal that stopped heavy; its folder, session, PID and parent
processes; its lane, its flags and how it got in (its lane's slot, sharing beside the
holder it names, or outside the queue); and the CPU-seconds it used and that other work
used on its cores meanwhile. Of the command it keeps only the first words, up to the
first that may be a value (one holding `=` or `@`, one after an option, or an option
other than a bare -X or --word) and 120 characters, since a command line can hold a
token; a bare positional token (`./deploy prod TOKEN`) is still kept. Its end line also
holds CMD's PID, which --overlapping matches as it does heavy's. A heavy inside a job
adds no record of its own. The record keeps its newest 2000 lines, about a day's jobs,
and lives with the queue, so a reboot that clears /tmp clears it. One that can't be
written is a note on stderr, never a failure. `heavy history` lists the jobs that
started in the last hour, or since TIME; --overlapping, the jobs that ran beside PID's
latest job, at TIME, or between START and END. A TIME is ISO (2026-11-24T23:16),
HH:MM[:SS] (the latest such moment), or an age (90s, 30m, 2h, 1d).

`heavy waits` prints one line: the p50, p90 and longest wait for a turn (from joining
the queue to starting, by the record) of the jobs that started in the last 30 minutes,
or since TIME, every session's, in both lanes or the one --lane names; then how many
jobs wait in the queue now and how long the oldest has waited. A --few-seconds job
that ran beside a slot, outside the queue, waited for no turn: those are left out and
counted, since they outnumber the rest and would pull the figures down. Each job still
waiting counts in p90 and max at its wait so far, a lower bound, so a jam shows before
any job starts; p50 counts only the jobs that started. With fewer than --min-jobs
jobs started (default 10), it says "too few jobs for percentiles" and gives max
alone, since with 5 jobs one wait from a throttled spell is p90 and max both. --json
prints one object instead, with seconds as numbers: `since` (epoch), `lane` (null for
both), `started`, `min_jobs`, `outside_left_out`, `p50`, `p90` (null with nothing or
too few to count), `max` (null with nothing to count),
`waiting`, `waiting_by_lane` (`normal` and `long`, whatever --lane says) and
`oldest_waiting` (null with none waiting).

`heavy waits --until-open` takes that reading every 30 seconds (HEAVY_WAITS_POLL) until
the queue is open: p90 under --p90 seconds (default 180; no p90, with nothing or too
few jobs to count, is under, so the waiting count alone decides) and at most --waiting
jobs waiting (default 1). It exits 0 then, or 3 once
--max-wait seconds have passed with the queue still held (default: no limit). It prints
two lines, its first reading and its last, each `open: ` or `held: ` and then the line
`heavy waits` prints, or one when the first reading is also the last (open at once, or
--max-wait 0); --json prints the object instead, with `open` added. An agent that
can't wait on its own clock, such as an overnight workflow's spawn gate, runs it in
the background and waits for it to exit.

`heavy waits --until-quiet` waits, the same way, for no job running or waiting in
either lane, from any session (holders, sharers, and --few-seconds jobs running
outside the queue, which the record shows), with the 1-minute load average under
--load (default 2), as before an end-of-night timing run: empty lanes alone said
nothing of the agents' own load, and one such run started at a load of 4.4 to 6.5.
Each reading is a line, `quiet: 0 running, 0 waiting, load 1.20 (under 2)` or `busy:
...`, first and last as --until-open prints them; --json prints objects with `quiet`,
`running`, `waiting`, `load` and `load_under`. It exits 0 once quiet, or 3 past
--max-wait. It takes no --lane, --p90, --waiting, --since or --min-jobs.

A command that fails while /proc/vmstat's oom_kill count rises hears on stderr that the
out-of-memory killer may have taken one of its processes, and to run it again if the
failure looks odd.

A heavy started inside a job (HEAVY_SLOT names the job's slot, or a --few-seconds job
run outside the queue while its heavy lives) runs at once, so a check that calls heavy
never waits on itself. A job whose process has ended is cleared from the queue, and so
is an entry whose first line isn't whole, as a crash can leave one. Stopping heavy
(SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP) stops CMD's process group, with SIGKILL after HEAVY_GRACE
seconds, and frees its place; CMD also gets SIGTERM should heavy die.

As a job ends, heavy measures its running time, without its wait in the queue,
against the perf promises of its repo and llm-toolbox's (perf-promises -h): a run that
took twice its promise or more hears so in one line on stderr, `heavy: NAME ran 312 s,
2.6x its promised 120 s (REPO/perf-promises.toml): perf-promises status`, or on a slow
machine `heavy: NAME ran 3425 s (1370 s at the machine's 0.40x speed, measured 2 min
ago), 11.4x its promised 120 s (...)`, and every run a promise matches goes on its
log. A promise file
it can't read is that line instead; neither changes CMD's status.

heavy takes the queue's lock for a moment at each look, and a Ctrl-Z that comes while
it holds it stops heavy only once it lets go. A heavy that has waited
FIFO_LOCK_SECONDS for the lock names the process holding it on stderr, and when that
process is stopped, how to continue it.

Environment:
  HEAVY_DIR            the queue's folder, with its history and record (default
                       /tmp/heavy)
  HEAVY_QUIET_SECONDS  how long the cores must stay quiet (default 10)
  HEAVY_POLL           seconds per sample (default 1)
  HEAVY_SHARE_SECONDS  how long a holder must idle before anyone shares (default 90)
  HEAVY_STALL_SECONDS  how long a head waits before suggesting a PR (default 600)
  HEAVY_QUICK_SECONDS  the window a --few-seconds job's cores must average quiet over
                       (default 2)
  HEAVY_QUICK_BUSY     how busy, at most, they average for it to run outside the
                       queue (default 0.25)
  HEAVY_QUICK_MAX      seconds a --few-seconds job runs before it hears to drop the
                       flag (default 60)
  HEAVY_GRACE          seconds CMD gets to stop before SIGKILL (default 5)
  HEAVY_ALONE_OUTSIDE  cores of other work, on average, past which an --alone job
                       hears its timing may be off (default 0.5)
  HEAVY_ALONE_REPORT   a file an --alone job's seconds, own and other CPU-seconds and
                       speed at its start and end go to, as one line
  HEAVY_SPEED_PROBE    a file whose `SECONDS [LOAD]` stand in for the speed probe's
                       seconds and the load before it (tests; read only while
                       PERF_PROMISES_DIR is set)
  HEAVY_SPEED_AFTER_EXIT  0: start no speed probe after a job exits (tests; read
                       only while PERF_PROMISES_DIR is set)
  HEAVY_WAITS_POLL     seconds between `heavy waits --until-open`'s or
                       `--until-quiet`'s readings (default 30; the tests shorten it)
  HEAVY_LOADAVG        a file that stands in for /proc/loadavg (tests)
  FIFO_LOCK_SECONDS    seconds of waiting for the queue's lock before naming its
                       holder (default 5; the tests shorten it)
  HEAVY_BUSY           a file whose number stands in for how busy the cores are (tests)
  HEAVY_MEMINFO        a file that stands in for /proc/meminfo (tests)
  HEAVY_VMSTAT         a file that stands in for /proc/vmstat (tests)
  HEAVY_STAT           a file that stands in for /proc/stat, for an --alone job's
                       other work (tests)
  HEAVY_SCCACHE_CPU    a file whose number stands in for the CPU-seconds sccache's
                       server has used (tests)
  HEAVY_CORES_USED     a number that stands in for the cores a run used (tests)
  HEAVY_SLOT           set for CMD, to the slot it holds (for a --few-seconds job run
                       outside the queue, `outside` and the line naming its heavy)
  OWN_PIDS             set by own-pids inside its PID namespace, where heavy refuses
                       the shared queue (HEAVY_DIR unset or /tmp/heavy, through any
                       links): it would take other sessions' jobs, whose PIDs it can't
                       see, for ended ones
  HEAVY_SHARED_DIR     a folder that stands in for /tmp/heavy as the shared queue,
                       HEAVY_DIR's default and one refused inside own-pids, beside
                       /tmp/heavy itself (tests)

Exit status: CMD's own; 2 a usage error; 3 `heavy waits --until-open` or
`--until-quiet` reached --max-wait with the queue still held; 125 heavy couldn't use
its queue, or refused
the shared one inside own-pids; 126 CMD
couldn't be run (a folder, or not executable); 127 CMD wasn't found; 128+N stopped
by signal N.
"""

import argparse
import ctypes
import json
import os
import shlex
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / "lib"))
import heavyjobs
import heavyqueue
import machinespeed

STOPS = (signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP)
# Why --few-seconds goes with none of these.
QUICK_WITHOUT = {
    "--long": "a long run isn't seconds of work.",
    "--alone": "a --few-seconds job may run beside the lane's holder, so it can't"
    " time anything.",
    "--share": "a --few-seconds job already runs beside the lane's holder when its"
    " cores are quiet, and --share is for work without time limits, such as a build.",
}
PR_SET_PDEATHSIG = 1


class Stopped(Exception):
    def __init__(self, signum):
        self.signum = signum


def on_signal(signum, _frame):
    raise Stopped(signum)


def die_with_heavy():
    """In CMD, before it starts: get SIGTERM should heavy die, even by SIGKILL."""
    ctypes.CDLL(None, use_errno=True).prctl(PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, signal.SIGTERM)


def hand_terminal(group):
    """Make group the terminal's foreground group, so CMD can read it; a CMD stopped
    for reading before the handover (SIGTTIN) is continued."""
    old = signal.signal(signal.SIGTTOU, signal.SIG_IGN)
    try:
        os.tcsetpgrp(sys.stdin.fileno(), group)
    except OSError:
        pass
    finally:
        signal.signal(signal.SIGTTOU, old)
    try:
        os.killpg(group, signal.SIGCONT)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        pass


def stop(proc):
    """Stop CMD's process group: TERM, then KILL after HEAVY_GRACE seconds. Further
    signals are ignored meanwhile, so a second Ctrl-C can't cut the KILL short."""
    for sig in STOPS:
        signal.signal(sig, signal.SIG_IGN)
    grace = heavyqueue.knob("HEAVY_GRACE", 5)
    for sig, wait in ((signal.SIGTERM, grace), (signal.SIGKILL, None)):
        try:
            os.killpg(proc.pid, sig)
            os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGCONT)  # a stopped group can't act on TERM
        except ProcessLookupError:
            return
        try:
            proc.wait(timeout=wait)
            return
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            continue


def warn_if_slow(ran):
    """Tell a --few-seconds job that ran for more than HEAVY_QUICK_MAX seconds that
    --few-seconds is for runs of seconds: once, as it ends, so the line is in the tail
    of its output."""
    limit = heavyqueue.knob("HEAVY_QUICK_MAX", 60)
    if ran > limit:
        heavyqueue.note(
            f"heavy: this --few-seconds job ran past {limit:g} s. --few-seconds is for"
            " runs of seconds, such as a few tests: run ones like this, or the build"
            " before them, without it."
        )


def run(command, long, alone, share, quick):
    with heavyqueue.turn(
        long=long, command=shlex.join(command), alone=alone, share=share, quick=quick
    ) as job:
        job.pin()  # CMD inherits this process's cores
        terminal = sys.stdin.isatty()
        try:
            # heavy runs no threads, so preexec_fn is safe here.
            proc = subprocess.Popen(
                command,
                env=job.env,
                process_group=0,
                preexec_fn=die_with_heavy,  # noqa: PLW1509
            )
        except FileNotFoundError:
            print(f"heavy: no command {command[0]} to run.", file=sys.stderr)
            job.status = 127  # for its record; no run to remember, or OOM kill to tell
            return 127
        except OSError as error:
            print(f"heavy: can't run {command[0]}: {error.strerror}.", file=sys.stderr)
            job.status = 126
            return 126
        job.command_pid = proc.pid
        if terminal:
            hand_terminal(proc.pid)
        begun = time.monotonic()
        try:
            code = proc.wait()
        except Stopped:
            stop(proc)
            raise
        finally:
            if terminal:
                hand_terminal(os.getpgrp())
            if quick and not job.nested:
                warn_if_slow(time.monotonic() - begun)
        job.exited(code)
        return 128 - code if code < 0 else code


def history(argv):
    """`heavy history [--since TIME] [--overlapping PID|TIME|START..END]`."""
    reader = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="heavy history", description="The jobs heavy ran: see heavy -h."
    )
    reader.add_argument("--since", help="only jobs that started since TIME")
    reader.add_argument(
        "--overlapping",
        metavar="PID|TIME|START..END",
        help="the jobs that ran beside PID's latest job, or at TIME, or in a range",
    )
    args = reader.parse_args(argv)
    try:
        since = None if args.since is None else heavyjobs.moment(args.since)
        around = args.overlapping
        if around is not None:
            around = int(around) if around.isdigit() else heavyjobs.window(around)
    except ValueError as error:
        reader.error(f"can't read that time: {error}")
    print(heavyjobs.report(heavyqueue.base(), since, around))
    return 0


HELD = 3  # `heavy waits --until-open` or `--until-quiet` reached --max-wait
QUIET_LOAD = 2.0  # `heavy waits --until-quiet`'s default 1-minute load average limit


def load_average():
    """The 1-minute load average, from /proc/loadavg (or the HEAVY_LOADAVG file, the
    tests' stand-in)."""
    path = os.environ.get("HEAVY_LOADAVG") or "/proc/loadavg"
    return float(Path(path).read_text().split()[0])


def quiet_reading(load_under):
    """What `heavy waits --until-quiet` reads: the jobs running and waiting in every
    lane, the 1-minute load average, and whether that is quiet."""
    running, waiting = heavyqueue.jobs_now()
    load = load_average()
    quiet = running == 0 and waiting == 0 and load < load_under
    return {
        "quiet": quiet,
        "running": running,
        "waiting": waiting,
        "load": load,
        "load_under": load_under,
    }


def quiet_line(found):
    """`heavy waits --until-quiet`'s line for one reading."""
    limit = f"{found['load_under']:g}"
    against = (
        f"under {limit}" if found["load"] < found["load_under"] else f"{limit} or more"
    )
    verdict = "quiet" if found["quiet"] else "busy"
    return (
        f"{verdict}: {found['running']} running, {found['waiting']} waiting,"
        f" load {found['load']:.2f} ({against})"
    )


def waits(argv):
    """`heavy waits [--since TIME] [--lane normal|long] [--min-jobs N] [--json]`, or
    with --until-open, a wait for an open queue."""
    reader = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="heavy waits",
        description="How long jobs waited for their turn: see heavy -h.",
    )
    reader.add_argument("--since", help="jobs that started since TIME (default 30m)")
    reader.add_argument(
        "--lane",
        choices=heavyqueue.LANES,
        help="only that lane's jobs and waiters (default both)",
    )
    reader.add_argument(
        "--min-jobs",
        type=int,
        help=f"give p50 and p90 only when this many jobs started"
        f" (default {heavyjobs.MIN_JOBS})",
    )
    reader.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="one JSON object")
    modes = reader.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    modes.add_argument(
        "--until-open",
        action="store_true",
        help="read every HEAVY_WAITS_POLL seconds (30) until the queue is open",
    )
    modes.add_argument(
        "--until-quiet",
        action="store_true",
        help="read every HEAVY_WAITS_POLL seconds (30) until no job runs or waits in"
        " either lane and the load average is under --load",
    )
    gate = {
        "--p90": (float, 180.0, "open below this p90, in seconds (default 180)"),
        "--waiting": (int, 1, "open with at most this many waiting (default 1)"),
    }
    for flag, (kind, _, text) in gate.items():
        reader.add_argument(flag, type=kind, help=f"with --until-open: {text}")
    reader.add_argument(
        "--load",
        type=float,
        help="with --until-quiet: quiet below this 1-minute load average"
        f" (default {QUIET_LOAD:g})",
    )
    reader.add_argument(
        "--max-wait",
        type=float,
        help="with --until-open or --until-quiet: exit 3 when still held after this"
        " many seconds",
    )
    args = reader.parse_args(argv)
    if args.until_quiet:
        given = [
            flag
            for flag in ("--since", "--min-jobs")
            if getattr(args, flag[2:].replace("-", "_")) is not None
        ]
        if given:
            reader.error(
                f"{', '.join(given)} doesn't go with --until-quiet, which reads no"
                " past jobs"
            )
    if args.since is None:
        args.since = "30m"
    if args.min_jobs is None:
        args.min_jobs = heavyjobs.MIN_JOBS
    if args.min_jobs < 0:
        reader.error("--min-jobs must be 0 or more")
    if not args.until_open:
        given = [flag for flag in gate if getattr(args, flag[2:]) is not None]
        if given:
            reader.error(f"{', '.join(given)} goes only with --until-open")
    if not args.until_quiet and args.load is not None:
        reader.error("--load goes only with --until-quiet")
    if not (args.until_open or args.until_quiet) and args.max_wait is not None:
        reader.error("--max-wait goes only with --until-open or --until-quiet")
    if args.until_quiet and args.lane is not None:
        reader.error("--lane doesn't go with --until-quiet, which waits on both lanes")
    try:
        heavyjobs.moment(args.since)
    except ValueError as error:
        reader.error(f"can't read that time: {error}")
    slow = machinespeed.slow_warning()
    if slow is not None:
        heavyqueue.note(slow)  # on stderr: stdout stays one line, or JSON

    def reading():
        # An age (30m) slides with each reading; a moment stays put.
        since = heavyjobs.moment(args.since)
        waiters = heavyqueue.waiting_since()
        return heavyjobs.waits(
            heavyqueue.base(), since, waiters, args.lane, min_jobs=args.min_jobs
        )

    if not (args.until_open or args.until_quiet):
        found = reading()
        print(json.dumps(found) if args.json else heavyjobs.waits_line(found))
        return 0
    try:
        poll = float(os.environ.get("HEAVY_WAITS_POLL", "30"))
    except ValueError:
        reader.error("HEAVY_WAITS_POLL must be a number of seconds")
    if args.until_quiet:
        load_under = QUIET_LOAD if args.load is None else args.load

        def show_quiet(found):
            print(json.dumps(found) if args.json else quiet_line(found), flush=True)

        def quiet():
            found = quiet_reading(load_under)
            return found, found["quiet"]

        return until(quiet, show_quiet, args.max_wait, poll)
    p90 = args.p90 if args.p90 is not None else gate["--p90"][1]
    most = args.waiting if args.waiting is not None else gate["--waiting"][1]

    def show_open(found):
        if args.json:
            print(json.dumps(found), flush=True)
        else:
            verdict = "open" if found["open"] else "held"
            print(f"{verdict}: {heavyjobs.waits_line(found)}", flush=True)

    def opened():
        found = reading()
        found["open"] = heavyjobs.is_open(found, p90, most)
        return found, found["open"]

    return until(opened, show_open, args.max_wait, poll)


def until(read, show, max_wait, poll):
    """Take a reading, (found, ready) = read(), every `poll` seconds until it is
    ready; show the first and the last. 0 when ready, HELD past `max_wait` seconds
    (None: no limit)."""
    start = time.monotonic()
    found, ready = read()
    show(found)
    first = True
    while not ready:
        if max_wait is not None and time.monotonic() - start >= max_wait:
            if not first:
                show(found)
            return HELD
        time.sleep(poll)
        found, ready = read()
        first = False
    if not first:
        show(found)
    return 0


def main(argv=None):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--long", action="store_true", help="a long run: a quarter of the cores"
    )
    beside = parser.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    beside.add_argument(
        "--alone",
        action="store_true",
        help="never share a lane, as for a timing: no job runs beside this one",
    )
    beside.add_argument(
        "--share",
        action="store_true",
        help="may start beside a lane's holder, at nice 10, for work without time"
        " limits",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--few-seconds",
        action="store_true",
        dest="quick",
        help="seconds of work, such as a few tests: at once on quiet cores, else next",
    )
    # Its old name, which docs from before 2026-11-24 give; -h names only the new one,
    # which says how long such a job may run.
    parser.add_argument(
        "--quick", action="store_true", dest="quick", help=argparse.SUPPRESS
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "command",
        nargs=argparse.REMAINDER,
        help="status, speed, history [OPTIONS], waits [OPTIONS], or -- CMD [ARGS...]",
    )
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if os.environ.get("OWN_PIDS") and heavyqueue.is_shared(heavyqueue.base()):
        print(
            "heavy: refusing the shared queue inside own-pids' PID namespace (as"
            " mutate-check runs its command), where other sessions' jobs look ended:"
            " put heavy outside, `heavy -- mutate-check ...`",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 125
    if args.quick:
        for option, why in QUICK_WITHOUT.items():
            if getattr(args, option.removeprefix("--")):
                parser.error(f"--few-seconds can't go with {option}: {why}")
    if args.command == ["status"]:
        slow = machinespeed.slow_warning()
        print(heavyqueue.status() if slow is None else f"{slow}\n{heavyqueue.status()}")
        return 0
    if args.command == ["speed"]:
        print(machinespeed.table())
        return 0
    if args.command[:1] == ["history"]:
        return history(args.command[1:])
    if args.command[:1] == ["waits"]:
        return waits(args.command[1:])
    if args.command[:1] != ["--"] or len(args.command) < 2:
        parser.error(
            "expected `status`, `speed`, `history`, `waits` or `-- CMD [ARGS...]`"
        )
    for sig in STOPS:
        signal.signal(sig, on_signal)
    try:
        return run(args.command[1:], args.long, args.alone, args.share, args.quick)
    except Stopped as stopped:
        return 128 + stopped.signum
    except heavyqueue.QueueError as error:
        print(
            f"heavy: can't use its queue ({error}). Fix or remove it, then run again.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 125


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
